WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.930
- [Narrator] The Russia Ukraine crisis

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:05.110
is causing uncertainty
in natural case markets.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.220
As Western countries
weigh potential sanctions

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.800
against Moscow, the
future of Nord Stream 2,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.020
the Russian-built pipeline
in the Baltic Sea,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.420
has been brought into question.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.670
- When it comes to Nord Stream 2,

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.980
the pipeline that would bring natural gas

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.950
from Russia to Germany,

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.820
if Russia further invades Ukraine,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.120
it will not happen.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.670
- [Narrator] But in Europe, the
picture is more complicated.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.850
That's because about 40% of
the continent's gas imports

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.110
used to generate power
for heating, cooling

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.170
and for some industrial
processes come from Russia.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.928
- Europe absolutely has
huge interdependence

00:00:36.928 --> 00:00:39.000
with Russia on gas.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.790
And it really is an interdependence.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.060
- [Narrator] The pipeline is
awaiting approval to operate

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.330
from German and EU regulators.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.440
If granted, it will double the capacity

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.770
of an existing pipeline in the region.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.800
- People are saying you've
got the Nord Stream 1 link,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.900
why do you need a Nord Stream 2?

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.730
A second link in the eyes of critics

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.300
will increase the dependence

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.500
of Germany and Europe on
Russian energy exports.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.170
- [Narrator] Concerns
that supplies to Europe

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.890
will be affected by the tensions come

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.950
as European and Asian prices
for the commodity soured

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.850
amid winter supply shortages.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.480
So how does the rising demand

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.960
and tensions in the region
have rippling effects

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.660
on the global market,
including on US efforts

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.263
to plug the supply gap?

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.880
Officials in the Biden, Trump
and Obama administrations

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.560
have opposed Nord Stream 2 over fears

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.910
that it would enhance Moscow's
political sway in Europe.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.830
- We don't want Europe's energy supplies

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.290
to be dependent on Vladimir Putin.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.080
- [Narrator] Now fresh threats from the US

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.340
to halt the 765-mile-long
underwater system are under way

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.500
as Russia amasses troops and
weapons on Ukraine's border.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.530
- And as geopolitical
tensions are increasing

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:51.300
around Ukraine, Russia
invaded Ukraine already once

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.280
in 2014, annexed part of its territory,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.420
the Crimean Peninsula.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.920
- [Narrator] Sanctions
were placed on Moscow

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.120
from Western countries, including Germany

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.000
following that military action

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.760
but the financial penalties
didn't include gas.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.250
- Now the United States are saying

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.110
that this time around,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.210
we should push for broader sanctions

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.980
if Russia attacks Ukraine again

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.110
and we should include energy.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:18.690
So essentially, the pipeline
is at the middle of all this.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.390
- [Narrator] Russia has been
pushing for the pipeline

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.410
to be approved as gas in Europe

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.010
has been in short supply.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.660
That's partly because Gazprom,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.690
Moscow's state-run supplier,

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.210
refilled domestic inventories

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.190
and allowed its sizeable storage Europe

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.010
to run unusually low.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.360
President Vladimir Putin
said quick approval

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.840
of the Nord Stream 2 pipeline

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.000
would help rebalance the European market.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.280
Those comments exacerbated hawkish fears

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.460
that Moscow might be weaponizing energy.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.930
- A limited supply of
Russian gas in Europe

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.360
has added to the global demand for gas.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.830
And specifically, liquified natural gas

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.920
that can be shipped from other places.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.350
So the liquefying natural gas facilities

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.890
in the US just going at full tilt

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.890
to try and meet as much of
that demand as they could.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.510
- [Narrator] US gas prices have risen

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.830
because more gases is being exported.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.500
But the total production
of all existing facilities

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.780
can only export a maximum of around 12%.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.300
The rest is sold at home,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.530
keeping US prices mostly insulated

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.093
from international ones.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.670
Any US sanctions targeting
natural gas in Russia

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.450
could come at a cost for Europe,

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.520
which may struggle to
find quick alternatives

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.443
of the supply.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.790
However, those penalties are unlikely

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.730
to be put in place by President Biden.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.690
- It's more likely that
they will reach some sort

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.750
of compromise, and the
Germans are saying look,

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.540
if there is a Russian
aggression in Ukraine,

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.472
if the Russians march in again

00:03:41.472 --> 00:03:44.020
into their neighboring country,

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.993
then we will stop the pipeline.

